WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:08.541
SIERRA: We had been planning to do an episode
on international students, visas and the education

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:10.630
system for the past few months.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:14.549
It seems like an underreported topic that
people should know about.

00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:16.510
But then, it became part of the news.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:22.930
CBS: Students’ whose schools plan to offer
online only classes in the fall will have

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:27.210
to transfer or leave the country or face possible
deportation.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.650
CTV: Several prestigious American universities
are now suing the Trump Administration.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:35.880
MSNBC: The Trump Administration backed down,
but I don’t think it’s the end for what

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.350
we might see from the Administration.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:43.570
SIERRA: You could be forgiven for thinking
that student visas are a classic partisan

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:49.289
issue in American politics - liberals pushing
for cultural diversity and a more open society

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:54.000
while conservatives warn of dangers to national
security and American jobs.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.629
But the real story is quite different.

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:01.769
For decades Republicans and Democrats agreed
that a steady pipeline of the world’s best

00:01:01.769 --> 00:01:07.680
minds into American universities is essential
for our economy, innovation, and competitive

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.790
advantage with the rest of the world.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.470
It was a system that largely worked.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:17.799
The Trump administration has taken a different
perspective, and the pipeline of international

00:01:17.799 --> 00:01:23.790
students into American businesses, hospitals,
and research facilities has begun to constrict.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.110
As this happens, other nations have begun
picking up the slack.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:31.100
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It
Matters.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:36.939
Today, international students, and American
competitiveness, under threat.

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:43.570
SIERRA: Alright, so I'm a student in another
country, why do I want to come to school in

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:44.570
the United States?

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:51.909
ALDEN: I mean, if you look at the lists of
the world's 100 best universities, more than

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:54.240
half of those are in the United States.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.369
You know, if you come here and you graduate
with a degree from a top American University

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:04.759
that's worth a tremendous amount throughout
your life.

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:05.869
My name is Edward Alden.

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:09.390
I'm a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign
Relations.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:15.800
I'm also the Ross distinguished visiting professor
at Western Washington University in Bellingham,

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:16.800
Washington.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.220
SIERRA: Alden is a leading voice on immigration,
innovation, and US competitiveness.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.620
He spends a lot of time studying the contributions
of immigrants to the US economy.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.350
ALDEN: I mean, secondarily, even if you're
not coming to the best institutions, I mean,

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:39.209
the United States has a global reputation
as a dynamic, innovative place and if you're

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:42.480
a talented ambitious foreign student, it's
a place you want to be.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:49.510
I mean, finally education is an English and
English remains the universal language of

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:50.510
business.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.069
So, you know, to the extent that you come
out from an American education with a strong

00:02:55.069 --> 00:03:01.220
grasp of the English language, that's something
also going to be very helpful in your life.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:06.470
BRIMMER: There are liberal arts colleges,
there are research institutions, institutions

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:13.489
large and small that can cater to many different
types of learning and covering many different

00:03:13.489 --> 00:03:15.400
styles.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.760
My name is Esther Brimmer.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:24.680
I'm Executive Director and CEO of NAFSA, the
Association of International Educators.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:30.120
So bright students around the world know they
can come here and get a great education.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.709
And perhaps one of the most exciting things
is the dynamism of the American classroom.

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:40.680
That is very rare, that idea of having an
interactive classroom where students and professors

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.849
are talking to each other, where the learning
is shared, it's not just the professor lecturing.

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:50.700
And I'll tell you as someone who was a former
professor, I love that environment.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:55.629
And indeed, it's something where we even put
out a publication on introduction to the American

00:03:55.629 --> 00:04:00.659
classroom to help students get used to the
idea that they will have to defend their ideas.

00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:05.730
SIERRA: Ok, so you decide you want to come
to school in the U.S.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:07.401
How does the system actually work?

00:04:07.401 --> 00:04:10.019
What are the options for getting a visa?

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:15.940
ALDEN: Well, you need to be admitted to the
university that you wish to attend.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.030
So that's the same thing an American student
would face.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:22.280
You have to apply to the university and write
your admissions essay, all the things that

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.660
American students do when they want to attend
a university.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:32.161
If you are admitted, then you need to apply
for what's called an f1 student visa, the

00:04:32.161 --> 00:04:37.460
visa does not allow you to remain in the United
States permanently, does not allow you to

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:42.310
work for income with some exceptions while
you're in the United States.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:47.290
It's a temporary visa that permits you to
come here and study.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:53.600
SIERRA: Alongside the F-1, there are also
J1 visas, and a few other lesser-known options.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.380
Together they account for roughly 1.1 million
foreign students in the US.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.509
For anyone who has done it or helped someone
do it, you know that applying to college is

00:05:03.509 --> 00:05:05.040
not easy.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.260
Even with some assistance from family or a
guidance counselor.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:12.850
For an international student, it also means
travelling to an embassy or consulate in their

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:17.960
home country, and going through a meticulous
and costly process to get a visa.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.411
And it isn’t as though they get to breathe
a sigh of relief once they are accepted, or

00:05:21.411 --> 00:05:23.370
even after they arrive.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:28.220
BRIMMER: For many students, it may be their
first time out of their home country.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:34.090
Maybe their family has all saved up their
money to send their bright child to the US.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.250
That's a big commitment by the child of the
family.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:42.830
When they get to the United States, their
institutions, of course, comply with the law.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:48.720
And some people may not realize, but every
international student is actually, in effect,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.130
tracked by its institution.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:54.740
In other words, there is an official at each
institution that has to know where that student

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:57.570
is, and be able to file documentation on them.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:03.100
So unlike any other visitor to the United
States, business travelers, tourists, only

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:07.950
international students are actually recorded
and tracked with that level of detail.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:13.120
So the student has a lot of obligations, a
lot of costs, and their institutions have

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.960
additional obligations as well.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.040
It's a big commitment, a very big commitment,
but it's a life changing commitment.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.190
Tracy: It’s funny how wide eyed I was when
I first came to study in America.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.250
I dreamed about so many things, the freedom,
the opportunities.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:32.350
Lingua Marina: America, I think, is one of
the best countries in terms of having great

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:33.350
people teach students.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:34.720
NBC: The cultural exchange that you get in
the U.S., you don’t get it anywhere else

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:35.880
in the world.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:43.360
Education USA: After studying here, you can
go anywhere and do whatever you want to do.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.569
SIERRA: Okay, so you're here for let's say
the two or four potentially, I guess, eight

00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:49.569
years.

00:06:49.569 --> 00:06:51.509
You know, what happens once you graduate?

00:06:51.509 --> 00:06:56.150
ALDEN: You can say ‘thank you very much,
United States.

00:06:56.150 --> 00:07:01.280
I've enjoyed my time in my education here’
and you can go back to your home country.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.330
And that's what a lot of foreign students
do.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:11.610
If you want to stay and work for a US company,
the only real option is what they call the

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:18.870
H1-B Visa Program, which allows US companies
to hire foreign citizens who have at least

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.800
a bachelor's degree.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:26.650
That program is quota capped at 65,000 per
year and there's always more demand than there

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:33.650
is supply but they're an extra 20,000 spots
for foreign students graduating with advanced

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:36.760
degrees from American universities.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:41.690
So particularly if you're working in, you
know, engineering or technical or scientific

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:45.810
fields, there's a reasonably good chance that
you can find an American company that will

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.659
want to bring you on under an H1-B visa.

00:07:49.659 --> 00:07:55.210
SIERRA: The H1-B visa is a crucial part of
the international student pipeline - it’s

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:59.030
what allows them to remain in the U.S. and
join the workforce.

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:04.120
Many of these students go on to become citizens,
found companies, and create jobs.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:12.450
ALDEN: You could make a pretty strong argument
that foreign students are at the heart of

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:15.970
US success as-as the most innovative economy
in the world.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:18.410
I mean, they have brought tremendous benefits.

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.790
Maybe you fill in the history a bit.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:26.069
Basically, going back to the 1950s, the United
States began to encourage foreign students

00:08:26.069 --> 00:08:27.069
to come here.

00:08:27.069 --> 00:08:29.680
And a lot of that was essentially a diplomatic
initiative.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:32.459
We wanted to build up friends around the world.

00:08:32.459 --> 00:08:37.310
I mean, it's the same reason the Fulbright
program was created to send American students

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:38.490
and faculty abroad.

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:42.450
We thought that if foreign students came here,
and they studied in the United States, they

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:47.990
got to see what life in the United States
was like that that would bring benefits down

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:51.790
the road when these people went back home,
they would look favorably upon the United

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:52.790
States.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:59.279
I mean, there are dozens of foreign leaders
who were educated at universities in the United

00:08:59.279 --> 00:09:00.430
States.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:04.610
Former Secretary of State Colin Powell used
to say that these educational exchanges were

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:08.740
the best diplomatic program the United States
had, that it did more than anything else to

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:10.940
win us friends around the world.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:16.680
SIERRA: When international students go home,
they bring American soft power back with them.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:20.390
And that’s because they’ve had the chance
to see the U.S. with their own eyes, in all

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:23.550
its contradictions and diversity of values.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.680
Not just what’s seen on the news in their
home countries.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:28.610
Many of them, however, choose to stay.

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:31.680
And when they do, they often turn out to be
innovators.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:39.060
ALDEN: So the second big benefit to the United
States comes from the foreign students who

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:44.500
study here and then decide to stay and make
their careers in the United States.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:50.870
And the number of those has been growing substantially,
particularly in the so called STEM fields,

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:56.600
you know, science, technology, engineering
and math, that are so important to American

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:57.600
innovation.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:03.959
You go back to the late 80s US universities
were graduating about 25,000 foreign students

00:10:03.959 --> 00:10:05.490
in the STEM fields.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:09.870
Today, it's close to 125,000.

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:15.041
And the overwhelming majority of those students
remain and make their careers here in the

00:10:15.041 --> 00:10:16.041
United States.

00:10:16.041 --> 00:10:20.459
The retention rate is about 75%.

00:10:20.459 --> 00:10:25.730
And it's higher than that, importantly, for
Chinese and Indian students who are by far

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:31.050
the two largest cohorts of foreign students,
especially in the STEM fields, the retention

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.980
rate there for Chinese is about 90%.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:36.360
And it's pretty close for Indians.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:41.540
And you know, if you look, for instance, at
the history of Silicon Valley, almost half

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:49.300
of Silicon Valley companies had at least one
foreign citizen as their founders.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:52.560
And most of them went to American universities.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.829
BRIMMER: It is really fascinating to look
at the so called ‘unicorn companies,’

00:10:56.829 --> 00:11:02.370
the startups that are now valued at over $1
billion, that a quarter of them were founded

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:08.740
by international students, people who came
here to study and then eventually were able

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:10.300
to create businesses.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:15.460
They've directly contributed to the wealth
of the United States and the well being of

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:16.460
the United States.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:21.890
SIERRA: The data is conclusive about international
students creating jobs.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:26.450
Sometimes this happens on a small scale, other
times you can get someone like Elon Musk,

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:31.790
who founded PayPal, Tesla, and SpaceX after
studying in the U.S. A lesser-known but no

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:38.350
less important name is Noubar Afeyan, an international
student of biochemical engineering at MIT,

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:42.120
who went on to found 38 companies in his career.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:48.140
One of those companies, Moderna, helped develop
the first COVID vaccine to be tested in humans.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:52.950
And even before they graduate, international
students make an enormous economic contribution

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:54.050
through tuition.

00:11:54.050 --> 00:12:00.840
BRIMMER: In addition to the important intellectual
benefits, there are significant economic benefits

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:03.910
to having international students and scholars
in the United States.

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:11.440
According to our most recent study, international
students and scholars contribute $41 billion

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.459
to the United States economy.

00:12:13.459 --> 00:12:18.590
They account for over 458,000 jobs.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:21.839
And those contributions are spread across
the country.

00:12:21.839 --> 00:12:27.750
After all, many international students pay
full fare, that means that they in effect

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:32.100
are helping finance the American students
sitting next to them in the classroom.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.260
It's a real contribution and a real benefit
to the United States.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:39.830
SIERRA: Seems like a sweetheart deal for the
U.S, and yet for years we’ve been losing

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:43.480
our edge in attracting the best international
students.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:47.430
At the same time, attitudes toward them have
been growing more hostile.

00:12:47.430 --> 00:13:50.050
We’ll dive into all that and more after
a quick break.

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:55.389
So in July, as the US was grappling with the
COVID pandemic, another topic began making

00:13:55.389 --> 00:13:56.389
headlines.

00:13:56.389 --> 00:14:01.250
The Trump administration announced a plan
that would upend the lives of international

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.190
students studying in the US.

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:07.870
ALDEN: The original regulation said unless
you're taking an in person class, you're going

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:12.280
to have to return to your countries because
you know, you can do online from India as

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:15.130
well as you can do it from the United States,
I mean, forgetting that, you know, that's

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:18.421
on the opposite side of the world, and so
you’d be taking classes in the middle of

00:14:18.421 --> 00:14:19.421
the night.

00:14:19.421 --> 00:14:25.600
BRIMMER: We saw major universities file legal
cases to have this proposal stopped.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:28.769
And what was amazing to see was the outpouring
of support.

00:14:28.769 --> 00:14:32.990
You saw all sorts of different types of business
groups.

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:35.240
You saw educational groups, you saw social
groups.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:36.490
You saw religious groups.

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:42.360
You saw communities and institutions and people,
international students and scholars and their

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:47.440
American counterparts, students and scholars
saying, ‘we want to have international students

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.180
here, we want to be sure that they're welcome.

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:54.450
We want to be sure that even if their courses
go online, that they won't fall out of status’.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:59.649
And it was remarkable in such a short period
of time to see that kind of swelling up of

00:14:59.649 --> 00:15:02.760
support for international students and scholars.

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:09.060
CBS: The announcement by ICE fueled strong
criticism from students, colleges, universities

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:13.170
and lawmakers, including some republicans
typically aligned with the White House.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:16.149
MSNBC:What we’re simply saying to the Administration
is “Stop.”

00:15:16.149 --> 00:15:19.540
Democracy Now: It’s not the foreign students’
faults, and to be honest, they are one of

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:22.900
the most valuable assets that this country
has.

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:26.290
MSNBC: It’s not time to shun those who have
excelled in their fields simply because they

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.170
are not American born.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:34.250
ALDEN: I mean, I think some of that is this
recognition of the broad value that foreign

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.529
students have to the United States.

00:15:36.529 --> 00:15:43.800
But I think it also kind of galvanized this
awareness that we really haven't been treating

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:50.210
foreign students very well, in some ways for
a couple of decades, but particularly in the

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:51.320
last few years.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:53.339
They really are taken for granted.

00:15:53.339 --> 00:15:58.089
I think their contributions to the United
States are poorly understood.

00:15:58.089 --> 00:16:03.339
And so I think this action really did bring
a pretty strong, coordinated response.

00:16:03.339 --> 00:16:09.779
I mean, it was noteworthy that the administration,
largely, though not completely, backed down

00:16:09.779 --> 00:16:16.500
on the eve of the hearing for a lawsuit that
was spearheaded by Harvard and MIT.

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:21.950
And I think it's pretty clear the judge would
have slapped the administration down for this

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:22.950
order.

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:26.410
And so the administration largely though I
say, not completely, reversed course.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:30.800
So in the revised rule, those students will
be able to stay.

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:36.899
But new foreign students, those who are getting
visas for programs starting this fall, unless

00:16:36.899 --> 00:16:42.259
they have at least one face to face class,
they're not going to be admitted to the country.

00:16:42.259 --> 00:16:46.959
And so there are going to be a lot of foreign
students who were hoping to attend in the

00:16:46.959 --> 00:16:50.670
fall of 2020 who are not going to be allowed
to attend under this new rule.

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:57.240
So there's going to be a significant decline
in new foreign students as a consequence of

00:16:57.240 --> 00:16:58.240
that rule.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.920
And at the moment, it doesn't look like the
universities are going to challenge this,

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:03.410
they're going to live with a compromise.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:08.600
SIERRA: This decline could affect the entire
pipeline - fewer students beginning their

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:13.110
studies could mean fewer students moving on
to Silicon Valley, fewer starting businesses

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:15.450
that could employ Americans during a recession.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:19.730
And while the July action and the backlash
against it were dramatic, it’s important

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:22.699
to realize that the changes didn’t start
there.

00:17:22.699 --> 00:17:30.010
BRIMMER: So even before the pandemic, there
had been nearly an 11% decline in the number

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:33.110
of new students coming to the United States.

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:39.270
The travel bans instituted early in 2017 and
then the related issues to that have sent

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:43.360
a message to some international students and
scholars that they're not welcome in the United

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:45.770
States and that's been a real concern.

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:50.809
That sends a message, not only to those from
specific countries cited in the executive

00:17:50.809 --> 00:17:53.230
order, but to all students.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:58.200
[They] began to wonder ‘if this country's
on the list, could my country be next?

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:01.650
Would that mean that maybe I can't get a visa?’

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:07.730
SIERRA: As U.S. policy becomes more severe,
places like Canada, the UK, New Zealand and

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:10.460
Australia have gained a competitive edge.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:17.190
In 2018 and 2019, as the pipeline was constricting
in the U.S., Canada saw double-digit growth

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:18.950
in its international students.

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:24.020
It’s already starting to reap the benefits
in terms of job creation, university income,

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:25.600
and innovation.

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:33.130
ALDEN: The situation seemed pretty stable
when President Trump came to office in 2017.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:38.010
Under the Trump administration, there has
been quite a sharp turn of direction.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:42.980
The-the administration has a different view
about foreign students than you know, what

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:45.620
has been the strong consensus here for decades.

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:50.380
Their belief is, first, that foreign students
are taking positions in the universities away

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:51.550
from American students.

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:55.730
If a foreign student’s being admitted, that's
an American student who doesn't have a seat

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:56.980
at the institution.

00:18:56.980 --> 00:19:00.260
Therefore that's a negative for Americans.

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:05.980
Also quite a strong conviction in the administration
that when foreign students remain and go to

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:11.890
work for US companies, they're also taking
jobs away from American citizens.

00:19:11.890 --> 00:19:20.190
So the administration has been doing a number
of things to try to discourage foreign students.

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:23.930
The student visa process has become longer
and more onerous.

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:29.770
There'll be limited work opportunities for
foreign students after they graduate.

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:34.750
Administration has been curtailing in various
ways the H1-B program, which is so important

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:36.900
for foreign students who want to stay and
work.

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:41.820
SIERRA: As the Trump administration rolls
back options for international students to

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:47.070
study and work in the US, misinformation about
them has been spreading.

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:50.650
Sometimes answers to even the most basic questions
are hard to find.

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:55.130
So, we asked Edward Alden to field four of
the most common ones.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:20:01.400
SIERRA: Are foreign students taking up slots
at schools that would otherwise go to American

00:20:01.400 --> 00:20:02.400
students?

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:03.520
ALDEN: Sometimes.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:07.960
The research suggests that at the elite institutions,
probably yes.

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:12.000
You know, if you're at a Stanford where the
acceptance rate is, I don't know 6% or 3%,

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:15.200
or whatever, there’s no question, sometimes
foreign students are getting places that are

00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:17.080
not going to American students.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:21.390
On the whole, the research suggests that the
more foreign students we have in the United

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:25.440
States, the more places in total there are
for Americans.

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:30.250
They may be at other institutions, but the
economic benefits from foreign students make

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:34.390
the universities stronger, they're able to
expand, and therefore there are more places

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:35.390
in total.

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:39.470
SIERRA: So the pie gets bigger as opposed
to just you know, getting devoured with none

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:40.470
left.

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:43.940
ALDEN: The pie on the whole gets bigger, but
you know, Stanford and Harvard and Yale don't

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:45.049
get any bigger.

00:20:45.049 --> 00:20:46.289
SIERRA: Okay.

00:20:46.289 --> 00:20:49.990
After students graduate, are they stealing
American jobs?

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:51.580
ALDEN: Again, mostly, no.

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:56.820
I mean, the evidence is that foreign students
because of the companies they start after

00:20:56.820 --> 00:21:01.309
they go to work are creating a lot more jobs
than they're taking away.

00:21:01.309 --> 00:21:03.380
In some places, yeah, definitely.

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:08.120
I mean, sometimes, you know, a foreign student
gets hired where an American doesn't.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:13.690
But in the aggregate, far more jobs created
for Americans, by the activities of these

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:16.669
students when they stay, then jobs that are
somehow taken away from Americans.

00:21:16.669 --> 00:21:18.370
So there too the pie is getting bigger.

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:24.150
SIERRA: Are spies from authoritarian countries
like China posing as foreign students in order

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:28.970
to steal sensitive information or technology
that could then be misused?

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:31.540
ALDEN: This is a complicated one, right?

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:35.900
Because, you know, Chinese students are the
single largest cohort of foreign students

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:37.210
in the United States.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:43.679
But you also have this growing economic, technological
and military rivalry between the United States

00:21:43.679 --> 00:21:44.890
and China.

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:50.501
And there's absolutely no question that the
Chinese have sent over researchers, that there

00:21:50.501 --> 00:21:55.650
are Chinese students who are acting effectively
as, as, as spies here in the United States

00:21:55.650 --> 00:22:02.130
trying to glean secrets from companies, trying
to recruit people in the United States, to

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:09.580
spy on the United States, trying to use every
tool they have to gain an advantage in the

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:12.530
competition between China and the United States.

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:19.940
So there is absolutely a need for targeted
interventions to try to identify these security

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:20.940
concerns.

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:22.640
Arrest people where appropriate.

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:27.160
Make sure that American Universities are aware
of this threat.

00:22:27.160 --> 00:22:31.539
But there's a big difference between that
sort of targeted response and the kind of

00:22:31.539 --> 00:22:35.150
blanket actions the administration is considering.

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:40.290
It's been talking about, you know, an across
the board ban on Chinese students who are

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:46.580
working in science and technology fields,
and that would just be tremendously costly

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:49.820
to the United States and an absolute shot
in the foot.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:53.039
So, yes, Chinese espionage is a real concern.

00:22:53.039 --> 00:22:58.840
No, the solution is not to bar Chinese students
from coming to the United States, that would

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:03.430
be an action whose consequences are far worse
than the problem.

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:09.340
SIERRA: So what about the students who come
here, get a good American education and then

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:10.890
take those skills home?

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.240
Do you think that's hurting the United States?

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:16.390
ALDEN: I mean, by and large, no.

00:23:16.390 --> 00:23:18.690
Again, the research here is pretty clear.

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:24.360
I mean, if they go back and work in companies,
you can see actually far closer relations

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:29.429
between companies headed by foreign students
who studied in the United States and the United

00:23:29.429 --> 00:23:30.429
States.

00:23:30.429 --> 00:23:33.770
They're inclined to, you know, buy components
from the United States, to export products

00:23:33.770 --> 00:23:36.250
to the United States, to engage in trade with
the United States.

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:40.840
I mean, the only potential area of concern,
getting back to the previous question is if

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:47.310
they're learning things that may be useful
to foreign militaries, or other things that

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:49.409
could somehow be used against the United States.

00:23:49.409 --> 00:23:53.539
But to some extent, it's sort of a fool's
game to try to control that kind of knowledge.

00:23:53.539 --> 00:24:01.289
I mean, in the modern world, information,
ideas, knowledge flow, very, very freely.

00:24:01.289 --> 00:24:09.070
SIERRA: So how do you think the student visa
issue has intersected with overall immigration

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:11.150
policy under the Trump administration?

00:24:11.150 --> 00:24:17.809
ALDEN: Yeah, this is a really interesting
question because it marks the biggest difference

00:24:17.809 --> 00:24:24.580
between the Trump administration and previous
administrations, both Republican and Democratic.

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:29.680
The immigration debate in the United States
for about three decades now has been largely

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:33.130
about legal versus illegal immigration.

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:37.860
And there's been a strong consensus in both
parties that legal immigration is an enormous

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:39.179
net benefit for the United States.

00:24:39.179 --> 00:24:40.270
It's a good thing.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:43.289
We want people coming here legally, we want
them to move to the United States.

00:24:43.289 --> 00:24:46.840
We want them to contribute to our economy
and our society.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:52.090
The problem is illegal immigration, and that
was, you know, strong consensus kind of from

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:53.100
the mid 1990s.

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:59.059
And so most of what Congress tried to do,
mostly failed to do but tried to do, was was

00:24:59.059 --> 00:25:03.450
come up with reforms that would be generous
on the legal immigration side, but would do

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:06.279
more to crack down on illegal immigration.

00:25:06.279 --> 00:25:11.279
The Trump administration has a different view
of the world, they think immigration generally

00:25:11.279 --> 00:25:14.059
is not a good thing for the United States.

00:25:14.059 --> 00:25:17.560
And so where this intersects with the student
visa discussion is, you know, if you're in

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:21.640
favor of legal immigration, you're inclined
to see these foreign students as kind of the

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:22.660
cream of the crop, right?

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:27.080
These are the most highly educated people
coming from around the world.

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:31.880
They're advancing their education at our excellent
institutions, and these are going to be the

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:35.990
legal immigrants who are going to make the
largest contributions to the economy.

00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:40.429
And so you had in both parties generally a
lot of enthusiasm for the program.

00:25:40.429 --> 00:25:44.760
Under the Trump administration, it's much
more ‘No, these foreign students are unwanted

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:46.649
competition for Americans.’

00:25:46.649 --> 00:25:51.100
And particularly as a result of Coronavirus,
which has, of course has done so much damage

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:56.910
to the economy and pushed unemployment well
up into the double digits, the administration

00:25:56.910 --> 00:26:02.789
is using that as a rationale for saying, ‘Well,
now in particular, we really need to keep

00:26:02.789 --> 00:26:08.010
immigrants out and we need to save the jobs
that are being created for Americans’.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:16.510
So the administration has used the pandemic
as a pretext for further cutting back on legal

00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:17.510
immigration.

00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:21.500
I mean most people haven't really noticed
this because it's happened through a whole

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:26.640
series of actions, but legal immigration to
the United States is about half of what it

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:28.380
was four years ago.

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:33.340
So students are not the only group being affected
by this at all.

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:39.149
BRIMMER: Several years of policies that projected
an anti-foreigner message take their toll.

00:26:39.149 --> 00:26:42.090
The rise in xenophobia takes its toll.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:49.460
If there continue to be policies that come
out that threaten their ability to comply

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:55.380
with the status or create such ambiguity that
they don't know whether they'll be able to

00:26:55.380 --> 00:27:02.130
study that will send a message to the world
that the US does not want to have international

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:03.130
students.

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:04.289
And that's not the case.

00:27:04.289 --> 00:27:07.490
People on campuses do want to have international
students.

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:14.360
But if they see national policies that say
the opposite it makes it very, very hard for

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:17.570
educators to welcome the students the way
they would like to.

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:25.590
ALDEN: The US is losing the status it had
as unquestionably the number one destination

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:27.780
of choice for foreign students.

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:34.929
All of the political and immigration and visa
hassles that have been exacerbated by this

00:27:34.929 --> 00:27:39.420
administration are sending a very clear message
to foreign students that, you know, if you

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:42.620
want to go to the United States, you want
to stay there, it's going to be a very difficult

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:43.620
journey.

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:46.659
So why not look for easier options in other
places?

00:27:46.659 --> 00:27:50.720
SIERRA: So, do you think that we can recover
our reputation?

00:27:50.720 --> 00:27:53.030
Or is the damage already done?

00:27:53.030 --> 00:27:55.679
ALDEN: That is a fabulous question.

00:27:55.679 --> 00:27:58.360
And you know, I'm going to give an annoying
‘I don't know.’

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:04.270
If you look at them at the modern history
of this, the resilience of the United States

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:05.560
is pretty extraordinary.

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:09.550
I mean, we did you know, we did a lot of fairly
unpleasant things to foreign students after

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:10.550
9/11.

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:15.730
There were some damaging things that were
done because of our fear in the immediate

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:18.010
aftermath of the attacks.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:22.370
But you had the Bush administration at the
time, which very much believed foreign students

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:26.720
were good for the United States trying to
fix those errors and put out the welcome mat

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:27.720
again.

00:28:27.720 --> 00:28:31.980
You know, I don't want to get particularly
partisan here, but if President Trump is reelected,

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:37.159
then I think you're going to see that as a
vindication for his protectionist policies

00:28:37.159 --> 00:28:39.789
on immigration and trade in other areas.

00:28:39.789 --> 00:28:45.780
And I think then we will really see a generation’s
worth of damage in terms of foreign students

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:46.799
coming.

00:28:46.799 --> 00:28:51.260
If there's a different outcome, I think the
US can still recover its ground here.

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:55.090
You know, you hinted at this at the outset,
in one of your questions.

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:59.669
I mean, I've mostly been talking about sort
of hard stuff like economic performance and,

00:28:59.669 --> 00:29:01.600
you know, the high politics of diplomacy.

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:09.460
But obviously, there are just tremendous benefits
to the mixing of young people from other places,

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:12.480
you know, I mean, this is why we have a Peace
Corps program.

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:18.010
This is why, you know, you have the Fulbright
scholarships to send US students abroad, and

00:29:18.010 --> 00:29:20.620
it's one of the big reasons we want to bring
foreign students here.

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:25.470
You know, we want our young people to get
to know people from other cultures, to get

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:31.130
to know people that speak different languages,
to be exposed to different ways of looking

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:35.730
at the world, to make friends from other backgrounds
and other cultures.

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:41.490
And it's hard to quantify the benefits from
that but I think it's pretty obviously a good

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:42.490
thing.

00:29:42.490 --> 00:29:48.159
And I think that's something we really want
to continue to encourage as a country and

00:29:48.159 --> 00:29:58.190
nurture and I hope we are not going to keep
going in the wrong direction.

00:29:58.190 --> 00:30:06.130
SIERRA: Want to learn more about the future
for international students?

